          Fragment:md
            ## Installation

            Solid (like other JSX frameworks) uses NMBL's Vite plugin. Install it as a dev dependency:

            ```bash
            # Using npm
            npm install --save-dev @nmbl-lang/vite-plugin

            # Using bun
            bun add -d @nmbl-lang/vite-plugin

            # Using pnpm
            pnpm add -D @nmbl-lang/vite-plugin
            ```

            ## Configuration

            Add the NMBL plugin to your `vite.config.ts`. It must come **before** the Solid plugin, and you tell it to target Solid's JSX:

            ```ts title="vite.config.ts"
            import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
            import solid from 'vite-plugin-solid';
            import nmbl from '@nmbl-lang/vite-plugin';

            export default defineConfig({
              plugins: [
                nmbl({ jsx: { framework: 'solid' } }), // must come before solid()
                solid(),
              ],
            });
            ```

            ## Basic Usage

            Unlike Vue, Svelte, and Astro (which use a `<template lang="nmbl">` block), JSX frameworks use a **tagged template**. Import the `nmbl` tag and return ``nmbl`…` `` from your component. Solid signals are called as functions inside `${…}` holes:

            ```tsx title="App.tsx"
            import { createSignal } from 'solid-js';
            import { nmbl } from '@nmbl-lang/vite-plugin/tag';

            export default function App() {
              const [count, setCount] = createSignal(0);

              return nmbl`
                div#app
                  h1.title NMBL + Solid
                  button(onClick=${() => setCount(count() + 1)}) Clicked ${count()} times
              `;
            }
            ```

            The plugin compiles the tagged template to Solid JSX at build time. Interpolation uses `${expr}` holes — and because Solid is signal-based, you call signals where you read them (`${count()}`), keeping the markup reactive.

            ## Control Flow

            NMBL's `@if` / `@elseif` / `@else` and `@each` blocks compile to the JSX control-flow expressions Solid uses. Conditions and loop sources go in `${…}` holes (call your signals there):

            ```nmbl
            @if(${loggedIn()})
              p Welcome back, ${user().name}
            @else
              a(href="/login") Log in

            ul
              @each(item of ${items()} :key="item.id")
                li ${item.name}
            ```

            `@if(${cond})` compiles to a JSX conditional and `@each(item of ${items()})` to a `.map()` expression — the same primitives Solid's JSX uses. One JSX-specific rule worth knowing: when you use `:key`, the loop body must have a single root element (the key goes on it) — a multi-root `@each` body with `:key` is a compile error. NMBL's `@await` / `@key` / `@snippet` are Svelte-only and will error in the JSX target.

            ## Comments

            `//` comments are stripped from the output (dev notes); `//!` comments are rendered as real HTML comments:

            ```nmbl
            // dev note — never shipped
            //! becomes <!-- an HTML comment -->
            p Visible text
            ```

            You can also comment out individual props inside a tag's parens — handy while debugging:

            ```nmbl
            button(
              type="button"
              // disabled
              /* aria-label="Add" */
            ) Add
            ```

            ## Editor Support

            The NMBL VS Code extension highlights ``nmbl`…` `` tagged templates inside `.tsx`, `.jsx`, `.ts`, and `.js` files, so the markup reads as NMBL rather than a plain string.

          Fragment:md
            > **Note:** The NMBL language server (diagnostics, completions, go-to-definition) currently covers standalone `.nmbl` files and `<template lang="nmbl">` blocks in Svelte/Astro — not the JSX tagged-template form yet. You get syntax highlighting in Solid files today; for the richest tooling, factor shared markup into a `.nmbl` file. Vue remains the most complete for full type intellisense via its dedicated Volar plugin.

            ## Troubleshooting

          .troubleshooting:md
            ### Tagged template not compiling

            Make sure `nmbl({ jsx: { framework: 'solid' } })` is listed **before** `vite-plugin-solid` in your Vite `plugins` array, and restart the dev server after editing the config.

            ### Reactivity not updating

            Solid is signal-based — read signals by calling them where they are used (`${count()}`, not `${count}`). Reading a signal outside a `${…}` hole won't track it.

            ### Syntax errors with indentation

            NMBL uses indentation for structure. The first line of the template (inside the backticks) sets the base indent — keep it consistent (2-space steps, no tabs).

